Chapter: InsertAtGrowRt

 The input shape the corpus lacked: a list built by repeated `list-insert-at`
 rather than by `list-push`. Both primitives fill in place when the capacity
 allows, and both grow when it does not, but only `list-push` was doubling.
 `list-insert-at` allocated exactly one more slot than it needed, so the very
 next insertion found the list full again and copied it whole. n insertions
 then cost O(n^2) bytes on an allocator that never frees, which no
 correctness test can see because every one of those copies holds the right
 answer.

 Inserting AT the length is an append and shifts nothing, so what this
 measures is the growth policy alone rather than the memmove beside it. The
 count is set where the two forms part company on this bed rather than where
 they part company in principle: thirty thousand elements asks the quadratic
 form for about 3.6 GB, which the host granted, and the arm passed both ways
 and measured nothing. Fifty thousand asks for about 10 GB, past what a
 32-bit address space can hold at all, while the doubling form still asks for
 under a megabyte.

  build-list : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  build-list (acc) (i) (n) =
   if i == n then acc
   else build-list (list-insert-at acc (list-length acc) i) (i + 1) n

  grown : Integer -> Integer
  grown (n) =
   let l = build-list [] 0 n
   in list-length l + list-at l (n - 1)

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   print-line-uni (show (grown 50000))
  end
